  "htmlContent": "<h2>Introduction to Structured Query Language (SQL)</h2><p>Welcome to the world of databases! At the heart of nearly every application that stores and retrieves information—from social media feeds to online banking—is a powerful language called SQL. So, what exactly is it?</p><p><strong>Structured Query Language (SQL)</strong> is the standard language for managing and manipulating data in relational databases. Think of it as the universal remote control for databases, allowing you to communicate your instructions clearly, whether you want to create a data structure, add new information, or ask complex questions of your data.</p><h3>The Three Core Languages of SQL</h3><p>While we often refer to SQL as a single language, it's more helpful to think of it as a suite of specialized sub-languages, each with a distinct purpose. These are typically categorized into three main groups:</p><ul><li><strong>Data Definition Language (DDL):</strong> This is the 'blueprint' part of SQL. DDL commands are used to define, create, modify, and remove database structures like tables, indexes, and schemas. You use DDL to build the container for your data. The primary commands are:<ul><li><em>CREATE:</em> To build a new database object (e.g., a table).</li><li><em>ALTER:</em> To modify the structure of an existing object.</li><li><em>DROP:</em> To permanently delete an object.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Data Manipulation Language (DML):</strong> Once you have your database structure, you need to manage the data inside it. That's where DML comes in. These commands are used for adding, deleting, and modifying the actual data within the tables. The primary commands are:<ul><li><em>SELECT:</em> To retrieve data from the database.</li><li><em>INSERT:</em> To add new rows of data into a table.</li><li><em>UPDATE:</em> To modify existing data within a table.</li><li><em>DELETE:</em> To remove rows of data from a table.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Data Control Language (DCL):</strong> Data is valuable, and controlling who can see or change it is crucial. DCL commands are used to manage user permissions and access rights to the database. It's the 'security guard' of SQL. The primary commands are:<ul><li><em>GRANT:</em> To give a user specific permissions.</li><li><em>REVOKE:</em> To take away permissions from a user.</li><li><em>COMMIT & ROLLBACK:</em> Commands used to manage transactions and ensure data integrity.</li></ul></li></ul><h3>Summary</h3><blockquote>In essence, DDL builds the database structure, DML manages the data within that structure, and DCL controls who has access to it.</blockquote><p>Understanding this fundamental separation of concerns is the first step to mastering SQL. By knowing which type of command to use for a specific task, you can approach database management with clarity and confidence.</p><p>In the upcoming lessons, we will dive deep into each of these sub-languages, starting with the foundation: the Data Definition Language (DDL). Get ready to create your first tables!</p>",
  "pageTitle": "Introduction to SQL: DDL, DML, and DCL",
  "shortSummary": "Learn what SQL is and understand its three core sub-languages: Data Definition Language (DDL) for structure, Data Manipulation Language (DML) for data, and Data Control Language (DCL) for permissions."
